॰ ══════════════ R-15-1 OVER A WHOLE SOURCE — `W-304` ══════════════
॰ Whether this source holds a character doc 15 does not admit, and WHERE.
॰
॰ THE PASS THIS MIRRORS IS `lex.rs`'s, NOT `repertoire::check`'s. Pass 0 is
॰ strict and knows nothing of `आस्की` — `repertoire.rs` contains the word zero
॰ times. The refusal `W-304` observed comes from the LEXER, which carries a
॰ ratified exemption, and porting the strict pass instead would have refused the
॰ 1,724 Latin characters standing legally in ten `spec/*.sas` files today.
॰
॰ Derived from four sites in `lex.rs` and mirrored here:
॰   `:583` sets in-ascii on the word `आस्की` and in-jal on `जाल`;
॰   `:676` and `:720` CLEAR BOTH on a `॥`, as a head token or as a trailing
॰     mark — so `॥` closes the directive either way;
॰   `:591` checks the repertoire per WORD, and only when not in-ascii;
॰   `:600` narrows rather than disables it under `जाल`: twelve marks and the
॰     two ASCII letter classes, and nothing else.
॰
॰ ANSWERS AN OFFSET, NOT A BOOLEAN, because a gate that says only "no" cannot
॰ name the character it refused. The offset of the FIRST inadmissible code point
॰ is returned; a clean source answers `मूल ॱ दैर्घ्य`, one past the last octet and
॰ therefore never a real position. One number tells the two apart.
॰
॰ IT LIVES HERE AND NOT IN `पदविभाग`, because `अक्षरकोश` ALREADY IMPORTS
॰ `पदविभाग` (line 8) and the reverse edge would be a CYCLE. The first version
॰ of this put the walk in `lex.t1` calling `अक्षरकोशॱसङ्केतमूल्यम्`, and Stage 1
॰ of the fixpoint PRODUCED NO IMAGE while `no_module_is_reached_without_being
॰ _imported` named the cause. Here both halves are reachable with no new edge:
॰ this module's own decoder and predicate, and `पदविभाग`'s word splitting.
॰
॰ Every sign `पदविभाग` knows is three octets wide, so `पदविभागॱत्र्यष्टकसाम्यम्` — bounds
॰ checked, already here — spells `आस्की` in five comparisons and `जाल` in three.

